\title{Dream Logic, Finitary Information Geometry,\\and Algebraic Forgetting}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a model of signed reasoning in which contradictory evidence can coexist without entailing unrelated claims. Revision inserts a literal and retracts its opposite, giving a local, non-monotone, order-sensitive dynamics. Semantic consistency coincides with conflict-freedom in the graph of complementary literals, and revision preserves it. Finite states are interpreted as finitarily open subsets: they are closed under finite unions and intersections but fail arbitrary-union closure. We then study ordered experience streams. A compositional memory is a monoid homomorphism from a free monoid to a representation monoid. Every finite-state memory over a nonempty alphabet is lossy; erased streams form a submonoid; and the observable algebra is the quotient by observational indistinguishability. Targeted deletion satisfies the corresponding universal property. Algorithms and examples connect these results to editable information stores and finite-state summaries.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Information in practice may be partial, conflicting, revised, and compressed. Databases combine fallible reports, sensors disagree, and later evidence can replace earlier commitments. Classical consequence, in which a contradiction permits every conclusion, is poorly matched to a store whose contents represent accepted evidence rather than settled truth.

We isolate four phenomena. First, contradiction remains local. Second, revision retracts information and depends on order. Third, finite information fragments admit finite set operations but not arbitrary accumulation. Fourth, bounded or selective memory identifies ordered histories through an algebraic quotient.

The semantic model uses signed literals. Every atom has positive and negative forms, and a state may contain neither, one, or both. Entailment is membership. Revision removes the complement of a requested literal and inserts that literal. A complementary-attack graph connects this model to argumentation: its only edges join opposite signs of the same atom, making consistency exactly conflict-freedom.

Finite states form the family of finite subsets of the literal space. This family resembles an open-set system for finite operations, but on an infinite carrier it is not a topology: a countable union of finite sets may be infinite. We therefore use the term \emph{finitarily open} and treat failure of arbitrary-union closure as a boundary theorem.

Finally, ordered streams form a free monoid. A compositional memory is a homomorphism, and equal memory values define a congruence. Counting proves unavoidable collisions for finite representations; the first isomorphism principle identifies quotient classes with observable states. These structures are united by locality: contradiction concerns one atom, revision edits one complementary pair, finiteness bounds current support, and quotienting identifies only histories an observer cannot distinguish.

\section{Signed states and non-explosion}

\begin{definition}
Let $A$ be a set of atoms. Its literal space is
\[
\Lit(A)=A\times\{+,-\}.
\]
For $\ell=(a,s)$, the \emph{opposite} $\bar\ell$ has the same atom and the other sign. A \emph{belief state} is a subset $B\subseteq\Lit(A)$, and entailment is membership:
\[
B\models\ell \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \ell\in B.
\]
An atom $a$ is contradictory in $B$ if $(a,+),(a,-)\in B$. The state is \emph{consistent} if no atom is contradictory.
\end{definition}

The opposite operation preserves the underlying atom, has no fixed point, and is involutive:
\[
\operatorname{atom}(\bar\ell)=\operatorname{atom}(\ell),\qquad
\bar\ell\ne\ell,\qquad \overline{\bar\ell}=\ell.
\]
Consistency is a property of states, not a condition for their existence.

\begin{theorem}[Contradiction without explosion]
Let $a,b\in A$ be distinct and put $B_a=\{(a,+),(a,-)\}$. Then $a$ is contradictory in $B_a$, but $B_a\not\models(b,+)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both signs of $a$ occur by construction. Every member of $B_a$ has underlying atom $a$, while $(b,+)$ has underlying atom $b\ne a$, so it is absent.
\end{proof}

Thus inconsistency does not imply triviality. The condition $a\ne b$ is necessary: without it, the proposed conclusion might already be one side of the contradiction. The result gives a minimal paraconsistent semantics suitable for evidence stores; richer inference rules may be added only with care if locality is to remain intact.

\section{Local non-monotone revision}

\begin{definition}
For a state $B$ and literal $\ell$, revision by $\ell$ is
\[
R_\ell(B)=\{\ell\}\cup\bigl(B\setminus\{\bar\ell\}\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

This is a one-coordinate overwrite: remove the contrary sign, insert the requested sign, and leave all unrelated literals untouched.

\begin{theorem}[Acceptance and retraction]
For every $B$ and $\ell$,
\[
R_\ell(B)\models\ell
\quad\text{and}\quad
R_\ell(B)\not\models\bar\ell.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The target is explicitly inserted. Its opposite is deleted, and cannot be reintroduced as the target because $\ell\ne\bar\ell$.
\end{proof}

Revision is consequently idempotent at a fixed literal: $R_\ell(R_\ell(B))=R_\ell(B)$. It also leaves every literal over a different atom unchanged.

\begin{theorem}[Genuine non-monotonicity]
For $C_\ell=\{\ell,\bar\ell\}$,
\[
C_\ell\nsubseteq R_\ell(C_\ell).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The old state contains $\bar\ell$, whereas the revised state does not.
\end{proof}

Hence revision is not merely expansion. Its ability to remove a commitment is the mechanism of correction.

\begin{theorem}[Order sensitivity]
For every state $B$ and literal $\ell$,
\[
R_{\bar\ell}(R_\ell(B))\ne R_\ell(R_{\bar\ell}(B)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left side contains $\bar\ell$ and excludes $\ell$; the right side contains $\ell$ and excludes $\bar\ell$.
\end{proof}

Contrary updates obey a last-write-wins law. Updates at different atoms affect disjoint pairs and therefore commute, suggesting normal forms indexed by the final update to each atom.

\section{Complementary attack and invariant geometry}

\begin{definition}
Define complementary attack by
\[
\ell\rightsquigarrow k\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad k=\bar\ell.
\]
A state is \emph{conflict-free} if it contains no attacking pair.
\end{definition}

The attack graph is a disjoint union of edges, one between $(a,+)$ and $(a,-)$ for each atom $a$.

\begin{theorem}[Consistency--conflict-freedom equivalence]
A belief state is consistent if and only if it is conflict-free under complementary attack.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An inconsistent state contains both signs of some atom, which form an attacking pair. Conversely, an attacking pair is a literal together with its opposite, so its shared atom is contradictory.
\end{proof}

The equivalence depends on this specific attack relation and should not be generalized to arbitrary argumentation graphs.

\begin{theorem}[Consistency preservation]
If $B$ is consistent, then $R_\ell(B)$ is consistent for every literal $\ell$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At the atom underlying $\ell$, the opposite is removed before $\ell$ is inserted, so both signs cannot remain. Every other atom is unchanged; a contradiction there would already have occurred in $B$.
\end{proof}

For $n$ finite atoms, the consistent states form a simplicial complex. Each atom has three consistent local statuses---absent, positive, or negative---so there are $3^n$ faces. The $2^n$ maximal faces choose one sign at every atom. Revision moves through this geometry by fixing one coordinate.

\section{Finitary openness and its obstruction}

\begin{definition}
For a set $X$, a subset $U\subseteq X$ is \emph{finitarily open} if it is finite.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Finite lattice laws]
The empty set is finitarily open. Binary intersections and unions of finitarily open sets are finitarily open. More generally, if $\mathcal U$ is a finite family of finite subsets of $X$, then $\bigcup\mathcal U$ is finite.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The empty set has cardinality zero. An intersection is a subset of either finite operand. The union of two finite sets has cardinality at most the sum of their cardinalities; induction gives the finite-family claim.
\end{proof}

Every singleton is finitarily open. On a finite carrier this is the discrete topology, but infinite carriers expose a boundary.

\begin{theorem}[Union of singleton naturals]
\[
\bigcup_{n\in\N}\{n\}=\N.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every member of the union belongs to a singleton of a natural number. Conversely, each $m\in\N$ belongs to the singleton indexed by $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arbitrary-union obstruction]
Every singleton $\{n\}\subseteq\N$ is finitarily open, but their countable union is not finitarily open.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Singletons are finite. Their union is $\N$, which is infinite.
\end{proof}

Ordinary topologies contain the carrier and are closed under arbitrary unions. Thus finite subsets of an infinite set do not constitute a topology. They instead form a finitary lattice of manageable fragments.

\begin{theorem}[Finiteness preservation]
If $B$ is finite, then $R_\ell(B)$ is finite.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Deleting an element gives a subset of $B$, and adjoining one element preserves finiteness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Revision bridge]
If $B$ is finite and consistent, then $R_\ell(B)$ is finite and conflict-free under complementary attack.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use finiteness preservation and consistency preservation, then convert consistency to conflict-freedom by the equivalence theorem.
\end{proof}

This bridge has three readings: semantic consistency, combinatorial compatibility, and finite information capacity are all preserved by one local update.

\section{Experience streams and compositional memory}

Let $\Sigma$ be an alphabet. The finite words $\Sigma^*$ form the free monoid under concatenation, with empty word $\eps$.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{compositional memory} with representation monoid $(R,\cdot,1)$ is a map $M:\Sigma^*\to R$ satisfying
\[
M(uv)=M(u)M(v),\qquad M(\eps)=1.
\]
Its erased language and observational equivalence are
\[
K_M=\{u:M(u)=1\},\qquad
u\sim_M v\Longleftrightarrow M(u)=M(v).
\]
\end{definition}

The relation $\sim_M$ is a monoid congruence: equivalent left and right factors yield equivalent concatenations.

\begin{theorem}[Finite-memory loss]
If $\Sigma$ is nonempty and $R$ is finite, every compositional memory $M:\Sigma^*\to R$ identifies two distinct streams.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $a\in\Sigma$. The streams $\eps,a,a^2,\ldots$ are distinct, so $\Sigma^*$ is infinite. No map from an infinite set to finite $R$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Erased streams form a submonoid]
The set $K_M$ contains $\eps$ and is closed under concatenation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We have $M(\eps)=1$. If $M(u)=M(v)=1$, then $M(uv)=M(u)M(v)=1$.
\end{proof}

Complete erasure records only the identity fiber; the full congruence records every collision and can carry more information.

\begin{theorem}[Observable quotient]
The map
\[
\Phi:\Sigma^*/{\sim_M}\longrightarrow\img(M),\qquad \Phi([u])=M(u),
\]
is a monoid isomorphism.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It is well defined by the definition of $\sim_M$, multiplicative because $M$ is, and surjective by definition of the image. If $\Phi([u])=\Phi([v])$, then $M(u)=M(v)$, so $u\sim_Mv$ and $[u]=[v]$; hence it is injective.
\end{proof}

Finite memory therefore forces a nontrivial congruence class, erased inputs form a submonoid, and quotient classes are exactly observable memory states.

\section{Targeted forgetting}

Choose a retention predicate $\rho:\Sigma\to\{0,1\}$. Define $F_\rho:\Sigma^*\to\Sigma^*$ on generators by
\[
F_\rho(a)=
\begin{cases}
a,&\rho(a)=1,\\ \eps,&\rho(a)=0,
\end{cases}
\]
and extend multiplicatively. It deletes unretained symbols while preserving the order of retained symbols.

\begin{proposition}[Marked symbols are erased]
If $\rho(a)=0$, then the one-symbol stream $a$ belongs to $K_{F_\rho}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The deleting branch gives $F_\rho(a)=\eps$, the identity of the free monoid.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal property of targeted forgetting]
Let $G:\Sigma^*\to S$ be a monoid homomorphism. Assume
\[
F_\rho(u)=F_\rho(v)\Longrightarrow G(u)=G(v).
\]
Then there is a unique monoid homomorphism
\[
\overline G:\Sigma^*/{\sim_{F_\rho}}\to S
\]
such that $G=\overline G\circ q$, where $q(u)=[u]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $\overline G([u])=G(u)$. The assumption makes this independent of representatives, and multiplicativity follows from that of $G$. Every quotient class has a representative, so the factorization determines $\overline G$ uniquely.
\end{proof}

Consequently the quotient by targeted forgetting is isomorphic to the submonoid of retained outputs and is the most economical compositional domain through which every compatible summary factors.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{Local revision.} Store literals in a hash set. Remove $(a,-s)$ and insert $(a,s)$. Expected time and auxiliary space are $O(1)$ per update; a balanced tree gives $O(\log|B|)$ time. A history of $m$ updates costs expected $O(m)$.

\paragraph{Consistency checking.} Scan the state and record signs seen at each atom. Finding both signs reports inconsistency and, equivalently, a complementary conflict. For $n=|B|$, expected time and storage are $O(n)$.

\paragraph{Selective filtering.} Scan a stream of length $m$ and append exactly the retained symbols. Time is $O(m)$ and output space is $O(r)$ for $r$ retained symbols. Equality of two targeted memories is decided in $O(m+n)$ time.

\paragraph{Collision search.} Enumerate candidate streams and store the first stream observed at each finite representation. A repeated representation yields a collision. For $N$ candidates and evaluation cost $T$, expected time is $O(NT)$ and storage is $O(\min(N,|R|))$. Finite-memory loss guarantees eventual success under exhaustive enumeration.

\section{Examples and applications}

For atoms $a,b$, the state $\{(a,+),(a,-)\}$ is contradictory about $a$ but entails neither sign of $b$. Revising by $(a,+)$ gives $\{(a,+)\}$, and then revising by $(a,-)$ gives $\{(a,-)\}$. Any literals over $b$ survive because revision is local.

At finite stage $N$, the union of the first $N$ singleton naturals is $\{0,\ldots,N-1\}$ and has cardinality $N$. Every finite stage remains finitarily open; only the unbounded union leaves the family.

For targeted forgetting, retain \texttt{red} and \texttt{blue} but delete \texttt{noise}. Then
\[
F(\texttt{red,noise,blue})=F(\texttt{red,blue}),
\]
so distinct histories become observationally equal. A finite compositional memory may instead record length modulo $k$. Concatenation corresponds to addition modulo $k$; the empty stream collides with every stream of length divisible by $k$, and such streams form the erased submonoid.

Potential applications include inconsistent databases, editable knowledge bases, event logs with redaction, finite-state telemetry, and local conflict analysis. The framework carefully separates inconsistency from triviality, revision from accumulation, finite closure from topology, and complete erasure from general indistinguishability.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The signed-state semantics is deliberately extensional: it records what is accepted, not why. This makes non-explosion transparent and revision local. Richer consequence relations could connect atoms, but would require propagation controls to preserve paraconsistency.

Finitary openness must not be confused with ordinary topology. Its value lies precisely in displaying the missing arbitrary-union operation. Directed or ideal completion is a natural candidate for adjoining global states while retaining finite approximations.

Several conjectural directions follow. First, every finite revision history should have a unique last-occurrence normal form: the final sign mentioned for each atom survives, and untouched atoms retain their initial status. Second, finite consistent states and one-atom updates should form an oriented cubical geometry. Third, ideal completion of finite signed states should recover arbitrary signed states under a Scott topology. Fourth, omission of a literal should persist through every history that avoids its atom, even if another atom remains contradictory. Finally, maximal conflict-free faces and oriented revisions may determine the partition into complementary pairs up to relabeling.

The memory model complements state revision. A current state may discard update order and multiplicity, while a stream quotient says exactly which histories are observationally equal. The universal property guarantees that compatible downstream summaries depend only on the quotient class.

\section{Conclusion}

Contradiction can coexist without explosion when consequence is tied to supported literals. Revision accepts a new sign, retracts its opposite, fails monotonicity, and records update order. Complementary attack makes consistency exactly conflict-freedom, and local revision preserves this invariant. Finite states support all finite set operations but encounter a precise arbitrary-union obstruction.

For ordered histories, finite compositional memory inevitably loses distinctions. Erased streams form a submonoid, observational equality forms a congruence, and its quotient is exactly the observable memory algebra. Targeted deletion realizes the quotient universally. Together these results describe what can coexist, what may be retracted, where finite information reaches its boundary, and what remains observable after forgetting.

